---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ias/ias5.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Core Disclosure Principle" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Minimum Information Items" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Accounting Policy Disclosure" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Comparative Information" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Materiality's Early Role" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Why IAS 5 Was Absorbed into IAS 1" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IAS 5 vs Modern IAS 1" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="withdrawn"
    explanation="IAS 5 (issued 1976) established general principles for what information financial statements should disclose. It was superseded by IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements (originally issued 1997), which absorbed and substantially expanded its content."
    historical="It is retained here for historical reference and because its core insight — that the face of the primary statements alone is insufficient, and notes are an integral part of a complete set of financial statements — remains the foundational premise of IAS 1 today. For current presentation and disclosure requirements, always apply IAS 1."
    link="/ias/ias1"
    linkText="IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements"
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Establishing that disclosure itself is a distinct requirement</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 5 embodied an idea now so fundamental it is easy to forget it needed stating explicitly: financial statements must disclose sufficient information for users to understand an entity's position and performance, beyond the bare numbers on the primary statements — recognition and measurement alone are not enough; the Framework's understandability and relevance objectives require explanatory notes.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IAS 5 MAPPED TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Understandability</strong> — bare figures without accompanying explanation (accounting policies, breakdowns, contingencies) would leave users unable to properly interpret the statements</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">Relevance</strong> — establishing minimum information items ensured a baseline of decision-useful content across all entities, regardless of jurisdiction</span>
          <span><strong style="font-weight:500">A foundational, general standard</strong> — IAS 5 was less a set of technical rules and more a statement of principle that disclosure requirements exist and matter, later vastly expanded by IAS 1</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 5's core insight — that the face of the primary statements alone is insufficient, and notes are an integral, required part of a complete set of financial statements — remains the foundational premise of IAS 1 today.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">What IAS 5 established vs what IAS 1 later added</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Concept</span><span>Status</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Notes are an integral part of financial statements</span><span>Established by IAS 5; unchanged today</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Fair presentation override / going concern</span><span>Not addressed by IAS 5; introduced by IAS 1</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><span>Detailed structure of each primary statement</span><span>Not addressed by IAS 5; introduced by IAS 1</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-bottom:0;">ℹ️ IAS 5 was a relatively thin, principle-establishing standard — IAS 1 is a vastly more comprehensive successor.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IAS 5 covered</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 5 applied generally to all entities preparing financial statements under IAS, establishing that certain minimum information should be disclosed, either on the face of the statements or in accompanying notes.</div>
        <div class="note">This general applicability, with no industry-specific carve-outs, mirrors IAS 1's own universal scope today.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know (historical)</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Notes to the financial statements (original)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">Explanatory material accompanying the primary statements, considered an integral part of a complete set of financial statements.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Accounting policies (original)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The specific principles, bases, conventions, rules, and practices adopted in preparing and presenting financial statements.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Core Disclosure Principle</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Sufficiency, not merely compliance</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IAS 5 established that financial statements should disclose all information that is material and necessary to make the statements clear and understandable — not merely comply with a checklist of mandatory items. This "sufficiency" principle, going beyond mechanical compliance, is directly echoed in IAS 1's continuing emphasis on fair presentation as an overarching objective.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Minimum Information Items</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">An early checklist, later vastly expanded</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Name of the reporting entity, and whether the statements cover an individual entity or a group</span>
          <span>Reporting period covered, and the reporting currency</span>
          <span>Whether the statements are consolidated or for the individual entity alone</span>
          <span>Comparative figures for the preceding period</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">These basic identification items remain required today, though IAS 1 has added far more extensive minimum disclosure requirements (segment information equivalents, detailed line item breakdowns, judgement disclosures, etc.).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Accounting Policy Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">An early requirement to disclose the "how," not just the "what"</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IAS 5 required disclosure of the accounting policies used in preparing the financial statements — recognising that the same transaction could be accounted for differently depending on policy choices (e.g. inventory valuation method, depreciation method), and users needed to know which policies were applied to interpret the figures correctly. This requirement survives essentially unchanged as a cornerstone of IAS 1's notes structure today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Comparative Information</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The origin of the "prior period" disclosure requirement</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">IAS 5 established that comparative information for the preceding period should be disclosed for all amounts reported — an early recognition that a single period's figures, without comparison, provide limited insight into trends and changes. This requirement is identical in principle to IAS 1's current minimum one-year comparative requirement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Materiality's Early Role</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">A concept present from the very beginning</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Even in IAS 5, the concept of materiality governed which items warranted separate disclosure — immaterial items could be aggregated or omitted, while material items required separate presentation or note disclosure. This early materiality-based filter is the direct ancestor of the materiality concept embedded throughout the Conceptual Framework and IAS 1 today.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Why IAS 5 Was Absorbed into IAS 1</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">A natural consolidation as presentation standards matured</div>
        <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">As the IASC developed a comprehensive standard governing the overall presentation of financial statements (IAS 1), it made sense to fold general disclosure principles directly into that single standard, rather than maintaining a separate, freestanding "information to be disclosed" standard — IAS 5 was withdrawn upon IAS 1's original 1997 issuance.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required disclosures under IAS 5</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Entity name, reporting period, currency, and consolidation basis</span>
          <span>Accounting policies applied</span>
          <span>Comparative figures for the preceding period</span>
          <span>Contingencies and commitments not otherwise recognised</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement mattered most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Determining materiality — which items warranted separate disclosure</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Estimates underlying disclosed contingencies and commitments</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:8px;margin-bottom:0;">⚠️ IAS 5's relatively brief, principle-based content would be considered significantly under-specified by today's standards — IAS 1 today runs to dozens of pages of detailed, prescriptive presentation and disclosure requirements.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IAS 5 vs Modern IAS 1</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IAS 5 (1976) vs current IAS 1</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 5 (withdrawn)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">Current (IAS 1)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Core disclosure sufficiency principle</span><span>Established</span><span>Same principle, retained as "fair presentation"</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Structured primary statement formats</span><span>Not prescribed in detail</span><span>Detailed minimum line item requirements for each statement</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Going concern assessment</span><span>Not addressed</span><span>Explicit management assessment and disclosure requirement</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Fair presentation override</span><span>Not addressed</span><span>Explicit rare override provision for exceptional circumstances</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IAS 5's foundational principle endures; the detail, structure, and rigour of IAS 1 today represent decades of subsequent refinement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping (Historical, Under IAS 5)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Accounting policy disclosure</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Ancestor of IAS 1's own accounting policy note requirement</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Comparative period figures</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge bp">SOPL</span></span><span>Minimum one prior period, per the original requirement</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Contingencies and commitments</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Ancestor of IAS 37's disclosure requirements</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IAS 5's legacy connects across the IFRS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 1</span><span>The direct successor, absorbing and vastly expanding IAS 5's general disclosure principles</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 8</span><span>Governs accounting policy selection/disclosure in far greater detail than IAS 5 ever did</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>IAS 37</span><span>Successor for detailed contingency and provision disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Sufficiency principle", definition: "The requirement that disclosed information be adequate for users to understand the financial statements, beyond mere checklist compliance." },
      { term: "Comparative information", definition: "Figures for the preceding reporting period, presented alongside current period figures to show trends." },
      { term: "Accounting policies", definition: "The specific principles, bases, conventions, rules, and practices adopted in preparing and presenting financial statements." },
      { term: "Notes to the financial statements", definition: "Information in addition to that presented in the primary statements — providing narrative descriptions or disaggregation of items, and information about items that do not qualify for recognition." },
      { term: "Materiality", definition: "Information is material if omitting, misstating, or obscuring it could reasonably be expected to influence decisions that users make on the basis of the financial statements." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
